From c53834b0276c0412bbd2c95fd89abba9d718df40 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Walker <ben@nvidia.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Aug 2024 10:08:10 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 02/21] sock: collapse the three connect APIs into one
 asynchronous spdk_sock_connect

v26.05 announced that spdk_sock_connect, spdk_sock_connect_ext and
spdk_sock_connect_async would be replaced by a single API. Do that now.

The survivor is named spdk_sock_connect and takes the opts pointer plus the
completion callback. It is always asynchronous: the socket is returned before
the connection is established and the outcome is reported through cb_fn. The
synchronous variants are gone rather than kept as wrappers, so there is one
contract instead of three overlapping ones.

Because the public API no longer has a synchronous path, the sock module
vtable loses its separate connect/connect_async pair as well - impl->connect
now carries the callback and is the only entry point.

The connect state machine is driven by spdk_sock_is_connected(), by
spdk_sock_flush() and by reads on the socket; adding a connecting socket to a
poll group is not by itself enough to complete it. Callers that want to block
poll spdk_sock_is_connected(), which is what the removed synchronous
implementation did internally anyway.

Removing the synchronous path also means posix_sock_is_connected() is now
the only thing driving posix_connect_poller() to completion, which exposed
a latent error path: on a failed deferred group add the poller rederived
the error from errno, even though posix_sock_group_impl_add_sock() already
returns a negative errno (and -ENOTCONN, which errno never carries). If
errno happened to be 0 the poller reported success after closing the fd,
and the caller went on to poll() and recv() on -1. Propagate the returned
error instead.

The uring module completes the connection synchronously and only defers the
callback, and it fired that callback from _sock_flush() alone. A caller that
polls spdk_sock_is_connected() before adding the socket to a poll group never
reaches a flush, so the callback never fired - hello_sock -N uring hung
forever at "Connecting to the server". Fire it from both paths, which is what
posix does through posix_connect_poller().

